        "# 1. The Mathematics of Compound Interest\n",
        "\n",
        "The compound interest formula is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$\n",
        "A=P\\left(1+\\frac{r}{n}\\right)^{nt}\n",
        "$\n",
        "\n",
        "where:\n",
        "\n",
        "- \\(A\\) = final amount\n",
        "- \\(P\\) = initial principal\n",
        "- \\(r\\) = annual interest rate as a decimal\n",
        "- \\(n\\) = number of compounding periods per year\n",
        "- \\(t\\) = number of years\n",
        "\n",
        "### Examples of \\(n\\)\n",
        "\n",
        "| Compounding | \\(n\\) |\n",
        "|---|---:|\n",
        "| Annually | 1 |\n",
        "| Semiannually | 2 |\n",
        "| Quarterly | 4 |\n",
        "| Monthly | 12 |\n",
        "| Daily | 365 |\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, a 7% annual interest rate is represented as:\n",
        "\n",
        "$\n",
        "r=0.07\n",
        "$\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Continuous Compounding\n",
        "\n",
        "When interest is compounded continuously, we use:\n",
        "\n",
        "$\n",
        "A=Pe^{rt}\n",
        "$\n",
        "\n",
        "where \\(e\\) is Euler's number:\n",
        "\n",
        "$\n",
        "e\\approx2.71828\n",
        "$\n",
        "\n",
        "Continuous compounding represents the mathematical limit of increasingly frequent compounding."
